# The knowledge graph is for OUR project. Anything copied in from elsewhere
# is not ours to graph — it buries our own nodes under a foreign codebase.

# A full copy of WordPress, pulled in as an audit reference. 211 MB, and it
# was 56% of every node in the graph: our own 20% was the minority in a graph
# named after this repository. The real reference lives at
# C:\Users\Administrator\wp-core, outside this tree.
.arena-ref/

# Build output and dependencies. Anchored with a trailing slash so a future
# source package named `next/` or `node_modules/` at the repo root is not
# swallowed by the rule — the unanchored-directory bug from 2026-09-30.
#
# ONE wildcard rule instead of one line per variant: `.next-preview/` alone
# was not enough (2026-10-04 pulled 36k build-output nodes), and listing
# variants by hand lost again on 2026-10-06 when `.next-prod/` appeared and
# put 12.8k nodes (22% of the AST) of compiled output into the graph. The
# `.next*` glob covers every present and future dist-dir variant — prod,
# preview-3200/3300/3400, verify-*, whatever a future session invents.
frontend/.next*/
frontend/node_modules/
node_modules/

# Screenshots produced by the UI sweep and editor probes. They are tool
# output, not project content: they change on every run, say nothing about
# the code, and each one would cost an image extraction pass.
frontend/scripts/artifacts/

# Uploaded media and runtime logs are data, not source.
media/
backend/media/
logs/

# Retired modules and dead code, kept for the decision record and a possible
# downgrade. They are not part of the live project — graphing them would put
# deleted modules back in the graph under a current-looking name. Removals so
# far: approvals (2026-10-04), invoicing (2026-10-04). Anchored with a
# trailing slash so a future source file named `_archived` is not swallowed.
_archived/
